{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 37, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli after his death in 1152.\n\n**Early Life and Reign**\nRaymond III was born around 1140 to Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His mother was the daughter of King Baldwin II of Jerusalem and his wife Morphia of Melitene. Raymond III had a younger sister named Melisende who married the King of Armenia.\n\nRaymond III became the Count of Tripoli after his father's death in 1152. He was only around 12 years old at the time. His mother Hodierna acted as regent for him until he came of age.\n\n**The Battle of Inab**\nIn 1149 Raymond II was killed during the Battle of Inab against the Zengid forces led by Nur ad-Din. The battle was a disaster for the Crusaders and Raymond II was beheaded. Raymond III's mother Hodierna was forced to pay a ransom to secure her son's release from captivity.\n\n**The Regency of Amalric I**\nIn 1152 Raymond III's mother Hodierna died and Amalric I King of Jerusalem became regent for Raymond III. Amalric I was the brother of Baldwin III King of Jerusalem and the son of King Fulk of Jerusalem. Amalric I played an important role in the early life of Raymond III and helped him to consolidate his power as Count of Tripoli.\n\n**Marriage and Later Life**\nRaymond III married Eschiva of Bures the daughter of William of Bures Prince of Galilee. The marriage was arranged by Amalric I and helped to strengthen the alliance between the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the County of Tripoli. Raymond III died in 1187 without any children and the County of Tripoli passed to his cousin Bohemond IV of Antioch.\n\nRaymond III played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during his reign. He was a member o